  "wrapper_note": "`\"*\"` marks a wrapper kind whose span coincides with its only child. syn's `Stmt::Expr` is one: for a block's TAIL expression it spans exactly the expression, and we build no statement node there at all. The expression's own entry already checks what is there, so a second claim here would say nothing. It is a statement about syn's shape, not a way to silence a kind -- one entry uses it.",
  "attribute_note": "Every `Expr::*` entry lists `expression_statement`. syn folds an attribute on an EXPRESSION into that expression's span (`#[cfg(test)] { ... }` is one Expr::Block); our `expression_statement` is what carries attributes, and the inner expression starts after them. Same bytes, and the difference is where the attribute is attached -- which the boundary policy already declares from the other side."
}
